          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Jomo Kenyatta's Early Life</strong><br>\nJomo Kenyatta was born around 1897 in British East Africa, which is now Kenya. His birth name was Kamau Ngengi. He attended mission schools before traveling to Europe in 1929. During his time in Europe, Kenyatta studied in Moscow, London, and Germany, gaining exposure to global perspectives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kenyatta's Political Awakening</strong><br>\nKenyatta's political consciousness developed as he worked with anti-colonial activists. He wrote a book called \"Facing Mount Kenya,\" which presented Gikuyu society and criticized British colonialism. His work marked him as a prominent African anti-colonial voice.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Return to Kenya and Incarceration</strong><br>\nKenyatta returned to Kenya in 1946 after 15 years abroad. He became a teacher and then the leader of the Kenya African Union. His push for independence led to his arrest by British authorities in 1952, during the Mau Mau uprising, and he was imprisoned until 1959.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
